257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (The methodology follows a highly structured data processing and machine learning pipeline to ensure robust and honest evaluation metrics. Ground truth positive sequences were meticulously curated from the PAZy database and expanded via BLAST against external libraries under strict 90% identity and query coverage filters. To prevent data leakage and artificially inflated scores, a homology-aware train-test split was executed using MMseqs2, ensuring no protein cluster was divided across the training and testing sets. The predictive architecture utilizes frozen ESM-2 \(650M parameter\) protein embeddings coupled with downstream classifiers, specifically Random Forest and Logistic Regression algorithms. Furthermore, the training pipeline incorporates a tier-weighted approach to properly balance highly verified "Gold" curated data against homology-propagated "Silver" data.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
258 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (Evaluation on a held-out homology test set demonstrated exceptional separability, with both classifiers achieving near-perfect Precision-Recall and ROC Area Under the Curve \(AUC\) scores against broad Swiss-Prot baseline negatives. Specifically, the Random Forest model exhibited a precision of 1.0 with zero false positives at its training-frozen threshold. To validate the model's true robustness and ensure it was not simply identifying generic hydrolases, a rigorous Phase-4 stress test was conducted using reviewed EC 3.1.1 and 3.1.2 Swiss-Prot hydrolases as hard negatives. The system successfully maintained a 0.0 false-positive rate against these confounding enzymes. These results confirm that PlasticDeg successfully leverages deep learning representations to isolate plastic-degrading properties from general protein backgrounds, establishing it as a highly reliable and precise screening pipeline for novel bioremediation candidates.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
