338 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (The rapid accumulation of plastic waste has become one of the most pressing environmental challenges of the 21st century, necessitating innovative solutions for sustainable degradation. Conventional plastic disposal methods, such as incineration and landfilling, often result in significant ecological footprints and secondary pollution. Consequently, there is an urgent scientific focus on biological degradation pathways, particularly those involving microbial enzymes capable of breaking down complex synthetic polymers.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
339 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (The PlasticDeg project aims to address this need by developing an intelligent system for predicting the plastic-degradation potential of bacteria and fungi using advanced machine learning techniques. By leveraging state-of-the-art protein language models, specifically the ESM-2 \(Evolutionary Scale Modeling\) framework, this project seeks to bridge the gap between genomic data and functional enzymatic discovery. The system utilizes a curated database of known plastic-degrading enzymes, primarily sourced from the PAZy database, to train a robust classification pipeline. This pipeline integrates bioinformatics tools like BLAST for dataset expansion and MMseqs2 for homology-aware data splitting to ensure high-quality, non-redundant training. Ultimately, PlasticDeg provides a scalable and technically rigorous baseline for researchers to identify novel biocatalysts, contributing to the global effort of mitigating plastic pollution through biotechnology and intelligent computational screening.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
340 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 177 0 R
/T (1. INTRODUCTION)
/E (1. INTRODUCTION)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
341 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 177 0 R
/T (1.1. General Introduction)
/E (1.1. General Introduction)
/Pg 16 0 R
