>>
endobj
559 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 188 0 R
/E (The literature survey reveals a significant paradigm shift in bioinformatics, moving from traditional sequence alignment methods toward advanced Deep Learning \(DL\) architectures and Protein Language Models \(PLMs\). Studies leveraging models such as ProtBERT, ProtT5, and ESM-2 have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in extracting deep evolutionary and structural features directly from raw amino acid sequences. Approaches incorporating contrastive learning, convolutional neural networks \(CNNs\), and ensemble machine learning algorithms \(like Random Forest and SVM\) have consistently achieved high predictive accuracy \(often exceeding 85-90%\) and strong F1-scores in predicting Enzyme Commission \(EC\) numbers and identifying potential plastic-degrading candidates. Furthermore, AI-driven pipelines like PEZY-Miner showcase the viability of these computational models in drastically accelerating the discovery of novel plastic-degrading enzymes. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
560 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 188 0 R
/E (However, the survey also highlights critical limitations within the current research landscape. Many state-of-the-art transformer models require immense computational resources and struggle with class imbalances. Models specifically show decreased accuracy when predicting highly specific hierarchical levels, such as the 4th digit of EC numbers, or when dealing with rare enzyme classes. Additionally, the literature frequently notes that existing models often lack rigorous experimental validation and rely heavily on the availability of well-curated training data, leading to challenges with small or imbalanced datasets. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
