782 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 201 0 R
/T (The baseline evaluation compared the non-linear Random Forest \(RF\) ensemble against a scaled linear Logistic Regression \(LR\) model. Both models demonstrated exceptional separability on the primary test set, achieving a PR-AUC of ~ 1.0. At their respective frozen thresholds, both algorithms successfully maintained a perfect precision of 1.0 \(zero false positives on the 277 easy Swiss-Prot negatives\).)
/E (The baseline evaluation compared the non-linear Random Forest \(RF\) ensemble against a scaled linear Logistic Regression \(LR\) model. Both models demonstrated exceptional separability on the primary test set, achieving a PR-AUC of ~ 1.0. At their respective frozen thresholds, both algorithms successfully maintained a perfect precision of 1.0 \(zero false positives on the 277 easy Swiss-Prot negatives\).)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
783 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 201 0 R
/T (While both models were highly conservative regarding false positives, the Logistic Regression model exhibited a slightly higher recall boundary \(67.9%\) compared to the Random Forest \(52.8%\), successfully identifying 36 out of 53 test positives versus RF's 28. However, RF remains the preferred primary baseline due to its lack of reliance on feature scaling and its robustness to the highly non-linear nature of 1280-dimensional embedding spaces.)
/E (While both models were highly conservative regarding false positives, the Logistic Regression model exhibited a slightly higher recall boundary \(67.9%\) compared to the Random Forest \(52.8%\), successfully identifying 36 out of 53 test positives versus RF's 28. However, RF remains the preferred primary baseline due to its lack of reliance on feature scaling and its robustness to the highly non-linear nature of 1280-dimensional embedding spaces.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [28]
>>
