/P 204 0 R
/T (7.5.2 Interpretability Challenges and Model Limitations Despite the strong predictive capabilities and the successful Phase-4 stress test against hard negatives, the framework exhibits inherent limitations typical of deep transfer-learning architectures. Specifically, the system faces a severe biological interpretability challenge. While ensemble models like Random Forest inherently calculate and rank feature importance to explain their decision-making process, the input features in this pipeline are highly abstract. Because the inputs are 1280-dimensional latent tensors extracted from the 33rd layer of a transformer model, the "most important" numerical features driving the classification cannot be easily reverse-engineered into physical biochemistry.)
/E (7.5.2 Interpretability Challenges and Model Limitations Despite the strong predictive capabilities and the successful Phase-4 stress test against hard negatives, the framework exhibits inherent limitations typical of deep transfer-learning architectures. Specifically, the system faces a severe biological interpretability challenge. While ensemble models like Random Forest inherently calculate and rank feature importance to explain their decision-making process, the input features in this pipeline are highly abstract. Because the inputs are 1280-dimensional latent tensors extracted from the 33rd layer of a transformer model, the "most important" numerical features driving the classification cannot be easily reverse-engineered into physical biochemistry.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
808 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 204 0 R
/E (Figure 7.4: Normalized feature importances for the Random Forest classifier. Note that because the input features are 1280-dimensional latent embeddings from the ESM-2 model, individual features lack direct biological interpretability, serving as a limitation of the current transfer-learning approach.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [4]
>>
